WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Hello, I'm Julie Zhou, and my journey in product design has been quite a ride.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:11.000
In 2006, I joined Facebook as their very first design intern. I was young, excited, and honestly a bit terrified.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.000
By age 25, I found myself in a management position. That early leadership experience shaped everything that followed.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.000
I spent 14 years at Facebook, eventually becoming Vice President of Design,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.000
leading design for products used by billions of people around the world. Billions. Let that sink in.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.000
One of my biggest challenges was scaling the design team from just a handful of people to hundreds of designers.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.000
It's not just about hiring more people. It's about creating systems, processes, and a culture that enables great work at scale.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.000
After Facebook, I co-founded Inspirit.Work, an advisory firm where we help rapidly growing tech companies

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.000
develop their products. We focus on the messy, real challenges that come with hypergrowth.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.000
In 2021, I founded Sundial with my team. Our mission is to empower organizations and product developers

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.000
with data-driven decision-making capabilities. Data should make us smarter, not just busier.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.000
I've also written extensively about design and product development. My essay collection,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.000
"The Year of the Looking Glass," offers my perspective on building products and leading teams.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.000
These aren't academic theories. They're lessons learned from the trenches of real product development.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.000
My design philosophy centers on several key principles. First, design decisions must be informed by data and user insights.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.000
I'm not talking about letting numbers make decisions for you. I'm talking about using data to validate hunches

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.000
and challenge assumptions. The best designers I know are curious about the 'why' behind the numbers.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Second, great design scales through systems and processes, not heroics. Early in my career,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.000
I thought good design was about individual brilliance. Now I know it's about creating frameworks

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.000
that enable hundreds of people to make good decisions independently. Design systems aren't just style guides.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:05.000
They're decision-making frameworks that encode your values and principles into reusable components.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Third, designers should think like product managers. Understand the business. Know your metrics.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Grasp the market dynamics. Design isn't decoration. It's strategy made visible.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Fourth, build for billions, but start with one user's needs. This sounds contradictory, but it's not.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
When you deeply understand one person's problem, you often find patterns that apply to millions.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:35.000
The key is choosing the right one person to start with, someone who represents your core use case.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Fifth, design leadership is about enabling others. As a leader, your job shifts from

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.000
making the best designs yourself to creating an environment where others can do their best work.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:53.000
This means setting clear direction, providing constructive feedback, and removing obstacles.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:59.000
I'm also passionate about rapid iteration and learning. Iterate quickly, fail fast, learn faster.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Perfectionism is the enemy of progress. Ship something, measure the response, adjust, and ship again.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:11.000
At Facebook, we ran thousands of A/B tests. Not because we loved tests, but because we loved learning.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Each test taught us something about human behavior, about what resonates and what falls flat.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:23.000
When building design teams, I focus on creating cultures of feedback and psychological safety.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:29.000
People do their best work when they feel safe to take risks, to propose wild ideas, to fail and learn.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Design critiques shouldn't be about tearing down work. They should be about collectively making it better.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.000
I also believe strongly in mentorship and developing people's careers. Every designer I've managed,

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:47.000
I've tried to understand their aspirations and help them grow toward those goals. Sometimes that means

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:53.000
they grow beyond my team or even the company. That's okay. That's success.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Cross-functional collaboration is another area I'm passionate about. Design doesn't happen in a vacuum.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:05.000
The best products emerge from tight collaboration between design, engineering, product management, research, and data science.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Learn to speak everyone's language. Understand their constraints. Respect their expertise.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:17.000
When it comes to product design process, I advocate for starting with user problems, not solutions.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.000
It's tempting to jump to solutions. Resist that urge. Sit with the problem. Understand it deeply.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Talk to users. Watch them struggle. Feel their pain. Only then should you start ideating solutions.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Use research to understand behavior. Prototype rapidly to test hypotheses. Measure impact with clear metrics.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:41.000
And always think about edge cases and scale. What works for 100 users might break for 100 million.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Accessibility and inclusion aren't nice-to-haves. They're requirements. When you design for

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:53.000
people with disabilities, you often make the product better for everyone. Curb cuts help wheelchair users,

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:59.000
but also people with strollers, suitcases, and delivery carts. The same principle applies to digital design.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Through my advisory work, I help companies with product strategy, design team structuring,

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:11.000
decision-making frameworks, and design culture development. I also coach design leaders on executive presence,

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:17.000
stakeholder management, and strategic thinking. The transition from designer to design leader is hard.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Many people struggle with it. I want to help make that journey smoother for others.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Looking back, I'm incredibly proud of the impact we had at Facebook. We helped billions of people

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:35.000
connect with friends and family, discover new communities, build businesses, and express themselves.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:41.000
But I'm equally proud of the designers we developed, the processes we created, and the culture we built.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:47.000
My advice for aspiring design leaders? Focus on impact over aesthetics. Beautiful pixels matter,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:53.000
but meaningful outcomes matter more. Learn to measure and communicate your impact in business terms.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.000
Develop your strategic thinking. And never stop growing your empathy for users, your team, and yourself.
